(warning: this is long.)

Kyrie, the lawful human valkyrie, and her faithful pup Rabies set out
together to retrieve the Amulet of Yendor for Tyr.  Sadly, their long
friendship was torn apart sometime around turn 12 when Rabies fell
victim to a falling rock trap (RIP, boy).  The weaker of our two
remaining heroes plodded on alone.

The first few dungeon levels were fairly uneventful; Dlvl 3 held the
entrance to the Gnomish Mines but they were left for later.  Dipped
for Excalibur when I reached XP 5.  I found a +1 splint mail, some
gloves, and a silver dagger early on, which allowed me to #twoweapon
with AC -1 starting around turn 3500.  On Dlvl 6 (the Oracle), I found
myself in a tight spot with a couple killer bees whom I was not yet
prepared to face (I still lacked all resistances).  At about half
health, I zapped a wand that had been engrave-ID'ed as "something that
makes things disappear" in the direction of the bee next to me; he
turned invisible.  "I know, why don't I zap myself with it?"  I
thought.  Good plan.  Turns out that zapping yourself with a wand of
make invisible is not a temporary effect.

Thankfully, a gray unicorn on the next level provided me with a horn
to restore the stat points drained by the bees' poison.  Several turns
later, I discovered and entered Sobokan.

If you never done it before, Sokoban is *not* fun when your
character's position is denoted only by a blinking cursor.
Thankfully, I only lost 3 or 4 luck due to invisibility-induced
mishaps.  In the zoo on the top level I gambled and ate a tengu -
teleportitis!  I had this fantastic intrinsic with no teleport control
for approximately the next **35000** turns.  This turned out to be a
blessing in disguise - I couldn't have asked for a better learning
tool.  Having teleportitis taught me just how important it is to move
carefully about the dungeon.

My Sokoban prize was a bag of holding; I have yet to get the "oR
ending.

Having acquired poison resistance from a delicious giant beetle while
shuffling boulders around, I figured that the time had come to go do
the Mines, sleep resistant or not.  I returned to the Oracle level,
crushed the bees that had caused me problems when I was an unskilled
two-weaponier, climbed to Dlvl 3 and headed downstairs to start
bashing gnome skull.  I hit a sleeping gas trap or two on the way
down, but fortunately they were not the cause of severe harm.

I rolled into Minetown, still invisible, and found a cross-aligned
temple.  I have a few YASDs logged trying to convert this altar at an
early stage, so I merely used it to B/U/C my piles of stuff.  I hadn't
grabbed a mummy wrapping yet and so the shopping spree would have to
wait.  My blinking cursor plodded/randomly teleported onward.

I cleared out the Gnome King's wine cellar with little difficulty
(aside from random teleports) and dug out the right side to loot the
luckstone and gems.  On my return climb through gnome-land, I was
pleased to discover that a co-aligned unicorn had taken to hanging
around Minetown.  I #named all my gems and threw them his way.  The
unicorn gracefully accepted my gifts and I left the Mines feeling
lucky.

Dlvl 8 was summarily filled with statues after I went ape with a
cockatrice corpse.  I know now that common dangers like pits and trap
doors are fatal to the wielder of a rubber chicken, and I was
extremely fortunate not to meet such a fate this time around.
Especially considering that when I switched back to my main weapons
and ditched the corpse on Dlvl 9, I promptly fell down a trap door to
the next level.

Dlvl 10: I explored the room I fell into, walked down a hallway,
opened a door and heard the most beautiful words in the English
language: "Welcome, daughter of Tyr!"  "You experience a strange sense
of peace."  I spent about 3000 turns within a dungeon level on either
side of this temple, clearing out monsters and sacrificing them.  On T:
15029 I finally acquired telepathy from a floating eye.  T:~16000 I
felt wide awake after gorging myself on a Grey-elf corpse.  T:17269 I
was crowned the Hand of Elbereth, and received fire resistance, shock
resistance, and see invisible for my troubles.  My body faded into
view.  Somewhere in this sacfest I scored a mummy wrapping and trawled
back to Minetown only to find that it basically sucked.  Izchak had
plenty of candles, but that was about it.  No hardware store to speak
of.

I began to explore further but returned frequently to my safe haven on
Dlvl 10 to pray for protection.  I managed to score a base AC of -1
through donations and prayer over the next 15,000 turns or so.
I also had a very scary near death experience on Dlvl 10.  While
moving (fairly) quickly through an unlit hallway, I ran smack dab into
a floating eye, who promptly paralyzed my ass.  A nearby cockatrice
sidled over and attacked.  I heard his hissing twice, was released
from my paralysis, and beat that chicken down like he'd killed my
parents.  I then ate a lizard corpse, which I probably didn't need to
do.

On Dlvl 12 I heard the call of the Norn, but did not answer until I
was ready to take on the quest.  Dlvl 16 held a neutral altar and a
general store.  The altar was quickly converted for convenience
purposes.  A throne on Dlvl 17 yielded nothing but a puff of logic.  I
explored down to Dlvl 21, clearing monsters and hoping for that
elusive =oTC, but no such luck was to be had.

A quick sidenote: uncontrolled teleportitis is great for finding and
looting vaults.  Ludios did not spawn in my game, but I made some
quick cash this way.

There was some luck to be had on a trip back upstairs, however, when I
quaffed from a fountain on Dlvl 19 and released a peaceful water
demon!  Thanks for the blessed +2 GDSM!  Around this point I also
realized I was really tired of kicking down doors all the time (I
hadn't found a key or even a lockpick yet) and so I paid a quick visit
to the Dlvl 16 general store with my trusty /oPoly, zapped, killed the
kobold shopkeeper and took his stuff.  Sorry buddy, thanks for taking
one for the team.

A wraith corpse had made me a warrior, and so I went to deal with Lord
Surtur.  The quest was a breeze; I had found an uncursed =oLev which I
used for much of it but did not need.  Still had to be careful with my
teleporitis (I didn't want to fall in any lava), but I cleared out the
hordes with little trouble, and sat on the upstairs to beat Surtur to
death once I figured out his (not very elaborate) ruse.  Hello, Orb of
Fate.

Equipped with everyone's favorite damage-reducing quest artifact, I
felt confident enough to head for the castle (though I still lacked
reflection).  Dlvls 24-27 were uneventful, though Dlvl 25 held a
barracks which supplied me with an abundance of rations and a bugle.
Dlvl 28 was Medusa, which I solved by the time-honored method of "dig
now, ask questions later."  I plopped into the Castle maze with AC
-17, 120HP, the Orb, and praying that I wouldn't run into an Arch-Lich
this time around.

Turns 38500-39000 were spent playing drawbridge smash.  A kind captain
gave me a silver saber which promptly replaced the silver dagger I had
been using until that point.

The castle wand was 0:2, and I opted to wish for the following items
(in order): 2 blessed !oCharging, "oR, blessed fireproof +2 speed
boots, a magic marker, a blessed fireproof +2 t-shirt, and blessed
rustproof +2 gauntlets of power (wrested).  Now that I have a little
more NH experience I would have wished differently here; I'm curious
to hear others' thoughts on this as well.

With my marker, I wrote a couple blessed genocide scrolls and punked h
and L (bastards).  I also burned ~45 charges writing enchant armor
(which I STILL hadn't IDed!) but in retrospect, this was a complete
waste.  I was able to milk the marker for a few more !oEA, but I would
have been better served writing !oGD instead (more on this later).

I stashed some stuff in the wand room, waltzed upstairs and dispatched
Medusa, broke apart the statues (no shield of reflection to be found),
and made a trip upstairs to take care of some unfinished business.  At
the temple on Dlvl 10 I made 13 potions of holy water.  At this point,
I still had two uncursed !oGenocide and wanted to reverse-geno tengu
and nurses.  Lacking holy water, I had the Brilliant Idea(TM) of
taking a few potions of water to the cross-aligned Minetown temple and
praying.

Hello unholy water, goodbye protection.  My AC dropped from -23 to
-12.

I briefly set up shop on the second floor of Sokoban, reverse-
genocided, and gained teleport control (finally) and about 65HP (I
didn't mind).  This put me at about 190HP.  I took a brief shot at
polypiling scrolls which yielded little of use, except for some
blessed ?oID and EW.  On the way back down to the castle, I noticed an
uncursed granite ring on the square adjacent to the altar in the
temple on Dlvl.  A quick ID revealed it to be teleport control.  I
have no idea how many turns it had sat there.

Back at the castle, armed with +6 Excalibur, a +6 silver saber, and AC
-14 (at this point I was wearing rings of +2 protection and free
action), I decided it was time to take on Gehennom.  I plunged into
the Valley of the Dead and began bashing face and grabbing treasures.
The first scroll I found was enchant armor.  RNG, you sly dog you!

You are standing at the gates of Gehennom.  Unspeakable cruelty and
harm lurk down here.  Are you sure you want to enter? [yn] (y) So be
it.

Gehennom was tedious.  I quickly learned to stash all scrolls and
potions immediately upon picking them up.  I also learned to be very
careful around green slimes.  While clearing the place out, I made
some long hauls back to Dlvl 10 to grovel for some of my protection
back; I was able to salvage only five points from my original 11.  A
scroll of genocide on Dlvl 32 was blessed and brought an end to the
existence of all water monsters.
Dlvls 34/35/36 were Asmodeus/Baalzebub/Juiblex respectively.  The
first two were killed while standing on the upstair, Juiblex took a /
oDigging and was promptly squished.  Applied my unihorn, levitated
around for a while looking for the downstair, and kept it moving.

(As a side note, what do people typically use as a light source down
here?  I used lamps and lanterns as I found them, but I spent a good
portion of Gehennom with a one-square visible radius.)

Dlvl 39 was Orcus-town and also had the entrance to Vlad's tower,
which I quickly cleared out.  Grabbed the "oLS and the Candelabrum.
Peace out.  I dug to Dlvl 40, was fortunate to find a cockatrice, and
returned to 39 to stone Orcus and loot his statue for the wand (0:4).

A quick telepathic look at Dlvl 42 revealed it as the summit of the
Wizard's tower!  I moved to 43 as soon as I found the downstair; I
didn't feel like stepping on any squeaky boards prematurely.

44-48 were uneventful except for a random genocide scroll that I opted
to use to gut disenchanters.  Somewhere down here I found a figurine
of a cockatrice that I saved for my ascension run.

On Dlvl 49, I felt a vibrating sensation underneath my feet.  I marked
the square with my bugle, which I was still trawling around for some
unknown reason, and then proceeded to map out the rest of the level
and patiently dig out paths between 19 levels' worth of stairs.  After
identifying a bunch of unknown rings at the VotD altar (including
conflict, thankfully) I levelported to Dlvl 1 and left my pickaxe
there with a bunch of K-rations.  I stopped at Sokoban on the way back
down to take one more shot at polypiling with my other /oPoly; this
time, I got nothing useful at all.  Moved my castle stash from the
corner tower to the center room (for easy access during my ascension
run) and I left a few blessed ?oRC and some holy water there just in
case.  I re-entered Gehennom, levelported to Dlvl 48, dug into the
Wizard's tower and began to climb.

I slaughtered everything on the way up; I didn't want to waste time
making my exit.  I arrived at Rodney's on Dlvl 42, took a deep breath,
dug in, and gave him a taste of my wand of death.  I picked up the
Book.  No turning back now.

Booked it out of the tower, levelported to the bottom of the dungeon,
vibrating square, invocation, "A staircase opens up at your feet!"
Off came the =oProtection (for the last time), on went conflict.  Down
we go.

Soon after arriving in the Sanctum, I was visited by Rodney for the
first time post-mortem.  Yes, I thought I couldst kill you.  I killed
him again, this time by just beating him to death.  I slaughtered many
of bunch of the monsters in the graveyard with the help of a random
purple worm with a taste for priest-meat... a good time was had by
all.  A /oSDD revealed the secret door into the Temple of Moloch.  The
high priest took two zaps from my trusty /oD, but once he went down I
grabbed the Amulet of Yendor (!) and cursed !oGL'd the hell out of
there.

The ascension run was pretty cool.  I thought I'd do most of it with
my saved rubber chicken, but when I (a)pplied my figurine, he spawned
tame.  Weak!  I killed him with wands, and he didn't leave a corpse...
thunder boomed above me, and I knew I'd be an atheist through the
Planes.  I ran into three more Rodneys (fortunately never more than
one at a time), and killed him the old fashioned way every single
time... I only had a single charge of death left, and I hadn't found
or polypiled a single ?oCharging in the entire dungeon.  The wizard
cursed some of my stuff (nothing important; I re-blessed my unicorn
horn).  The mysterious force was irritating but not devastating.
Safely out of Hell, I began ^T-ing my way upstairs whenever I was at
full Pw.  Back on Dlvl 1 I grabbed my pickaxe and food, dropped a
bunch of unnecessary items, and assessed by situation.  Some blessed
potions of full healing.  Unicorn horn.  An amulet of livesaving I
can't wear.  Only one scroll of gold detection.  Hoo boy...

I saved and took a break to install Jukka's dumplog patch.  I prayed
that my confidence would not be my undoing.  Back in NetHack, stairs
up, and it's time to take the final test.

The planes were extremely touch and go, and I wore the Amulet of
Yendor on Earth, Fire, and Water (I opted to save my ?oGD for Air,
which I had deemed the most dangerous based on everything I've read).

On Earth, I bashed Rodney's face in, killed a bunch of minotaurs and
earth elementals, and dug around until I found the cavern with the
portal.

On Air, I swapped conflict for levitation (and briefly, the Amulet of
Yendor for my much preferred "oR), confused myself by trying to cast
create monster (long since forgotten), and read my solitary gold
detection scroll to locate the portal.  I beelined for it; I was
engulfed a few times, but my HP never dropped more than about 40
points below max.

Fire took forever once I put conflict back on -- I nearly wore out my
Enter key paging through line after line of fire-resistant monsters
attacking each other.  I had a very tense near death experience when a
dust vortex engulfed me and dumped me in lava.  Fortunately, I had a /
oCold handy and was able to freeze the lava and dig myself out (thank
god I kept my pickaxe!)

Swapped conflict for levitation again on Water, which turned out to be
a complete cakewalk.  It took me a little bit to find the portal, but
when cobras and water elementals are your toughest competition, you
can afford to take some time.

I arrived on the Astral plane, where I was promptly met by a group of
hostile angels as soon as I swapped rings.  Of all the ridiculous
business that goes on on Astral, I was surprised by one subtle fact:
it was dark!  What the hell is this about?

I took a few turns to satiate my stomach, blind myself, and map my
plan of action.  I had arrived only the slightest bit left of center.
Famine was left, Pestilence up the gut, and death to the right.  I
headed left.  /oTele cleared my path, Famine was somehow killed by
conflicted monsters, and I entered the first temple.  Chaotic.  I left
the way I came and ran into Famine #2, who hit me with his DEVASTATING
FOOD DRAINING ATTACK.  I zapped him with the final charge from my /oD
and continued onward.  I didn't see Pestilence until he was right next
to me, whcih didn't really matter anyway as I had no means of killing
him aside from brute force.  He made me ill, but I quickly beat him
down and unihorned myself back to health.  I zapped my way straight up
to temple #2 and was exhilirated to find that it was the right one.  I
#chatted the high priest to be sure (take a look at my final gold
count) and offered the Amulet of Yendor to Tyr.

